**Proposition 17.4 (separately attained scout variants).** The following
schedules are also sufficient for this same historical task, with their
own actual acquired images. Let $a$ be the preprocessing Tick count,
$z=T^ax_h$, $\ell=(k_*-1)2^{-p_*}>0$, and $c$ the actual number of
scout Reads. The table counts only acquisition, before adding the
seed and historical bills.

| Schedule | Certificate and assay precisions | Actual return | Tick, Hold and digital Read counts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| One Tick, rational cutoff | $a=1$; scout $1,2,4,\ldots$; least $s$ with $\lambda^s<\ell$, then $U=s-1$; assay $p_r=3(U+r)+1$ | $T^{n+1}x_h$ | $n+1,\ n+1,\ c+b(n+1)$ |
| $n$ Ticks, rational cutoff | $a=n$; scout $0,1,2,4,\ldots$; least $s$ with $\lambda^s<\ell$; assay $p_r=3(s+r)+1$ | $T^{2n}x_h$ | $2n,\ n+1,\ c+b(n+1)$ |
| Alpha-skipping linear scout | $a=\delta=\mathbf1_{\{n>0\}}$; scout $0,1,\ldots,p_*$; $U=6(p_*+1)$; assay $p_r=3(U+r)+1$ | $T^{n+\delta}x_h$ | $n+\delta,\ 0,\ p_*+1+b(n+1)$ |

The first two rows Hold once before scouting and share that Hold with
assay epoch zero, then Hold after each assay Tick. In the second row,
the $n$ preprocessing Ticks precede this single Hold. In the last row
all diagnostic Holds are omitted; the seed Hold remains outside the
table. No autonomous evolution occurs while local work or Reads run.

Proof. For the first row put $R=Q+1$. Equation (17.6) gives
$\lambda^R\ge\ell$. Thus the least $s$ with $\lambda^s<\ell$
satisfies $s>R$, so $U=s-1\ge R$. The same inequality
$\lambda^{6p_*}<2^{-p_*}\le\ell$ gives $s\le6p_*$.
Its epoch-$r$ field lies on the $8^{-(U+r)}$ lattice, and the
precision $3(U+r)+1$ extracts it exactly by the proof of Theorem 10.2.

For the second row, the root coordinate of $x_h$ is at least $2^{-Q}$:
each historical Tick preserves at least one half of its old root
coordinate, and births preserve it. Every terminal has depth at most
$n$, because each birth raises material depth by at most one. In $n$
further Ticks, a path from root to that terminal, padded by diagonal
steps, has product at least $8^{-n}$. Therefore
$z_{\ell_j}\ge2^{-(Q+3n)}$, including alpha at $n=0$.
Its common denominator divides $8^{Q+n}$ and norm is at most
$\lambda^{Q+n}$. The scout terminates by precision $Q+3n+1$,
or the next listed power of two, so $p_*<2(Q+3n+1)$.
The least $s$ satisfies $Q+n<s\le6p_*+1$; the harmless added one
also covers a possible stopping precision zero. Extraction now uses
the lattice $8^{-(s+r)}$.

For the last row, $z$ is positive with denominator dividing
$8^R$, $R=Q+\delta$, also when $n=0,\delta=0$. Thus
$p_*\le3R+1$. Equation (17.6), and
$\lambda^{6(p_*+1)}<2^{-p_*}$, give $R<U$.
The same epoch extraction applies. Reads and local work do not change
the field, so omitting identity Holds changes neither sample nor
endpoint. Counting the actual Ticks, epoch Holds and Reads proves
the table. $\square$

For Construction 17.2 the scout precision sum is $2p_*-1$ and the
assay precision sum is $b(n+1)P$. The rational-cutoff doubling rows
have scout sum at most $2p_*$; the linear row has sum
$p_*(p_*+1)/2$. The cutoff search uses finite rational power products
and comparisons. Each row changes its own precision work, calls or
physical endpoint; no total optimum or combination of their cheapest
coordinates is asserted. Exact-Read A needs no scout when that stronger
port is genuinely available.

